# Payload fields exempted from the reader sweep (F1.4,
# instruments/field-consumers.py). One entry per line:
#
#   <payload-key> -- <why nothing reads it, and what would change that>
#
# ABSENT          -> no policy on record; the sweep excuses NOTHING and
#                    says so loudly (it cannot tell an exemption from an
#                    oversight).
# COMMITTED EMPTY -> a conscious "every payload field here must have a
#                    reader". Armed and silent.
# POPULATED       -> armed, and every entry is re-checked: an exemption
#                    for a field that has since acquired a reader, or for
#                    a field this ledger no longer carries, FAILS.
#
# THIS FILE IS DELIBERATELY EMPTY. ADR-046 already states the admission
# rule -- a payload field is admitted only if the fold or a blocking gate
# reads it -- so there is nothing to excuse: a field with no reader is a
# defect to fix, not a policy to record. The two legacy-admitted fields
# ADR-046 names, `blast_forecast` and `concerns`, need no entry here:
# both turn out to have real content readers (validate_events type-checks
# the forecast; instruments/concern-tag.py reads the tags), so the sweep
# passes them on their merits rather than on an exemption.
#
# One field is FAILING as this file is committed, and that is intended:
# `reaffirm_cleared` rides 1072 records and its only consumer is a
# presence test. F3.5 (wk-fadcc69d) is the fix -- the burials instrument
# reading prior_anchor and touched. Exempting it here instead would hide
# the exact finding the sweep was built to make.
#
